    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe create-owner endpoint is an OfficeFloor function pipeline, so I added the new business rule as one small step alongside the existing `EnsureUnique*` guards:\n\n1. **`EnsureCityUnderCapacity`** (new function) \u2014 rejects the request when its city already holds \u2265 50 owners, throwing a checked `CityAtCapacityException`. Runs before `BuildOwner` persists anything, consistent with the other guard steps that validate the request DTO up front.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException`** + **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** + escalation YAML \u2014 the handler responds **409 Conflict** with a `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the existing `DuplicateTelephone`/`DuplicateHousehold` handlers and reusing the shared `ProblemDetails` builder.\n\n3. **`Cities`** (new package-private helper) \u2014 extracted the \"count owners in a city\" logic. `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` already had a private `countOwnersInCity`, so I moved it into `Cities.countIn(...)` and now both that step and the new capacity check share it rather than duplicating the query.\n\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the `ensureCityUnderCapacity` step into the pipeline (after `ensureUniqueHousehold`, before `normalizeEmail`), under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbours.\n\nThe capacity threshold lives in one named constant (`EnsureCityUnderCapacity.CAPACITY = 50`), and each class keeps a single responsibility.",
